{"parentUuid":"9af120b8-0ebc-4e9f-9fe6-005d3d8f8121","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2d29f537e2b0ba30","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01Ptk9R2vcZBiSf41tivsLmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01V9WnNW58BNExs6QCrTCo83","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"CEM vs reinforcement learning vs evolutionary candidate ordering placement problems"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6372,"cache_read_input_tokens":20367,"output_tokens":240,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6372},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":240,"cache_read_input_tokens":20367,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6372,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6372,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoySNgeA764NfUjVD8BU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9117edf4-c63e-4936-977b-0f65e728cda3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:10:34.766Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"36ded915-beec-4969-b434-67656a9bdf19","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"a2d29f537e2b0ba30","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SM5SXzSUv3XHqD8e8FFk4g","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CEM cross-entropy neural combinatorial optimization 2025 benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"The Cross-Entropy Method: A Unified Approach to Combinatorial Optimization, Monte-Carlo Simulation and Machine Learning | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4321-0\"},{\"title\":\"Self-Improvement for Neural Combinatorial Optimization: Sample without Replacement, but Improvement\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2403.15180v1\"},{\"title\":\"Neural Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/neural-combinatorial-optimization\"},{\"title\":\"Amazon.com: The Cross-Entropy Method: A Unified Approach to Combinatorial Optimization, Monte-Carlo Simulation and Machine Learning (Information Science and Statistics): 9780387212401: Rubinstein, Reuven Y., Kroese, Dirk P.: Books\",\"url\":\"https://us.amazon.com/Cross-Entropy-Method-Combinatorial-Optimization-Monte-Carlo/dp/038721240X\"},{\"title\":\"A Tutorial on the Cross-Entropy Method Pieter-Tjerk de Boer\",\"url\":\"https://web.mit.edu/6.454/www/www_fall_2003/gew/CEtutorial.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Cross-Entropy Method for Combinatorial and Continuous Optimization | Methodology and Computing in Applied Probability | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1010091220143\"},{\"title\":\"Combinatorial Optimization via Cross-Entropy | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/302216608_Combinatorial_Optimization_via_Cross-Entropy\"},{\"title\":\"Calibration of second order traffic models using continuous cross entropy method - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0968090X12000277\"},{\"title\":\"A Guide to the Cross Entropy Method (CEM) – Dao of Sai\",\"url\":\"https://saiprasanna.in/posts/2024-03-cross-entropy-method/\"},{\"title\":\"Amazon.com: The Cross-Entropy Method: A Unified Approach to Combinatorial Optimization, Monte-Carlo Simulation and Machine Learning (Information Science and Statistics): 9781441919403: Rubinstein, Reuven Y. Y., Kroese, Dirk P.: Books\",\"url\":\"https://www.amazon.com/Cross-Entropy-Method-Combinatorial-Optimization-Monte-Carlo/dp/1441919406\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding CEM (Cross-Entropy Method) and neural combinatorial optimization:\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Cross-Entropy Method (CEM) is used as a problem-independent training scheme for neural combinatorial optimization, where solutions are sampled for randomly generated problem instances in each epoch using the current model.\n\n## Recent Applications (2024-2025)\n\nIn neural CO research, CEM involves sampling a set of solutions for problem instances using the current model, treating the sampled solution with the best objective function evaluation as a pseudo-expert trajectory for supervised training, and repeatedly 'self-imitating' the best solutions found to create an improving loop.\n\n## 2025 Benchmarking Context\n\nSelf-improvement approaches use the current policy to generate high-quality solutions as pseudo-labels, then retrain on these labels, often with sequence decoding schemes that enforce diversity (such as sampling-without-replacement). Additionally, recent work in 2025 focuses on constraint and risk modeling for robust optimization in real-world settings, along with standardized reporting on solution quality, computational resource use, and stability across benchmarks and problem variants.\n\n## Key Methods\n\nPreference-based training leverages dense pairwise supervision among multiple rollouts using objective differences to weight the loss, with best-anchored and objective-guided pairwise losses increasing sample efficiency by exploiting all generated solutions.\n\nThe search results show active development in combining CEM with neural networks for benchmarking combinatorial optimization problems, though specific 2025 benchmark papers weren't directly identified in these results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"ec5aa652-7f44-4a5b-b6ca-74af9cbd4ec7","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:10:41.452Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"36ded915-beec-4969-b434-67656a9bdf19","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9117edf4-c63e-4936-977b-0f65e728cda3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"a2d29f537e2b0ba30","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V9WnNW58BNExs6QCrTCo83","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CEM vs reinforcement learning vs evolutionary candidate ordering placement problems\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CEM-RL: Combining evolutionary and gradient-based methods for...\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=BkeU5j0ctQ\"},{\"title\":\"[1810.01222] CEM-RL: Combining evolutionary and gradient-based methods for policy search\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.01222\"},{\"title\":\"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2019 CEM-RL: COMBINING EVOLUTIONARY AND\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=BkeU5j0ctQ\"},{\"title\":\"Evolutionary Reinforcement Learning: A Systematic Review and Future Directions\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/13/5/833\"},{\"title\":\"Evolutionary Reinforcement Learning: A Systematic Review and Future Directions\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13296v1\"},{\"title\":\"CEM-RL: Combining evolutionary and gradient-based methods for policy search | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328040648_CEM-RL_Combining_evolutionary_and_gradient-based_methods_for_policy_search\"},{\"title\":\"Combining Evolution and Deep Reinforcement Learning for Policy Search: A Survey | ACM Transactions on Evolutionary Learning and Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3569096\"},{\"title\":\"Evolution Strategies | Lil'Log\",\"url\":\"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2019-09-05-evolution-strategies/\"},{\"title\":\"Sample-efﬁcient Cross-Entropy Method for Real-time Planning Cristina Pinneri∗1\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.06389\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] CEM-RL: Combining evolutionary and gradient-based methods for policy search | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/CEM-RL:-Combining-evolutionary-and-gradient-based-Pourchot-Sigaud/d9e08e872c69e5acfed2a93ec6f6d623cfd0c680\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CEM vs reinforcement learning vs evolutionary methods for candidate ordering and placement problems:\n\n## Key Comparisons\n\nDeep neuroevolution and deep reinforcement learning (deep RL) algorithms are two popular approaches to policy search. Evolutionary approaches are widely applicable and rather stable, but suffer from low sample efficiency. By contrast, deep RL is more sample efficient, but the most sample efficient variants are also rather unstable and highly sensitive to hyper-parameter setting.\n\n## The Cross-Entropy Method (CEM)\n\nThe CEM iteratively optimizes strategies through statistical methods, with a particular emphasis on elite selection to guide policy development. The Cross-Entropy Method (CEM) was shown to be a strong baseline in policy search problems.\n\n## Hybrid Approaches\n\nAn emerging approach consists in combining evolutionary methods with reinforcement learning so as to get the best of both worlds. For example, the CEM-RL combination uses the simple cross-entropy method (CEM) and TD3, an off-policy deep RL algorithm which improves over DDPG.\n\n## Application Considerations\n\nCEM for trajectory optimization is indeed a promising metaheuristics which has been shown to work well even with learned models, producing comparable or higher performance than model-free reinforcement learning methods. However, there is a problem intrinsic to population-based optimizers which makes these methods unsuitable for real-time planning and control: the high computational price. CEM requires a large number of samples to minimize the objective function.\n\nWould you like more specific information about any of these approaches or their applications to particular placement problems?\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a3fd881e-f467-4ba4-8e05-358012da6a3b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:10:42.057Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9117edf4-c63e-4936-977b-0f65e728cda3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9af120b8-0ebc-4e9f-9fe6-005d3d8f8121","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"ac68b9dc-9847-4e6f-8ec2-6b4367414408","agentId":"a2d29f537e2b0ba30","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VoFJPiknXSCmv1CykTHFa5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"discrete MCMC gradient sampling combinatorial optimization Sun 2023 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Reheated Gradient-based Discrete Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2503.04047\"},{\"title\":\"Revisiting Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/sun23c.html\"},{\"title\":\"Convergence Acceleration of Markov Chain Monte Carlo-based Gradient Descent by Deep Unfolding\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13608v1\"},{\"title\":\"Revisiting Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=ZMP0Bki9aK\"},{\"title\":\"Regularized Langevin Dynamics for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00277\"},{\"title\":\"Enhanced gradient-based MCMC in discrete spaces\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf?id=j2Mid5hFUJ\"},{\"title\":\"Markov chain Monte Carlo - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_chain_Monte_Carlo\"},{\"title\":\"[2503.04047] Reheated Gradient-based Discrete Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.04047\"},{\"title\":\"[2208.00040] Enhanced gradient-based MCMC in discrete spaces\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.00040\"},{\"title\":\"Gradient-based Discrete Sampling with Automatic Cyclical Scheduling\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.17699v1\"}]\n\nI found several highly relevant research papers on this topic. Here are the key results:\n\n## Key Papers (2023-2024)\n\n**1. \"Revisiting Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\" (Sun et al., 2023)**\n\nThis paper revisits using sampling for combinatorial optimization, motivated by significant advances in gradient-based discrete MCMC, and finds that modern sampling strategies can leverage landscape information to provide general-purpose solvers that are competitive with state of the art combinatorial solvers.\n\n**2. \"Gradient-based Discrete Sampling with Automatic Cyclical Scheduling\" (2024)**\n\nRecent advances have leveraged gradient information in discrete distributions to improve proposal distributions, setting new benchmarks in discrete sampling tasks across graphical models, energy-based models, and combinatorial optimization.\n\n**3. \"Discrete Langevin Samplers via Wasserstein Gradient Flow\" (Sun et al., 2023)**\n\nThis work by Sun et al. (2023) on discrete Langevin samplers via Wasserstein gradient flow represents a key contribution to gradient-based discrete MCMC methods.\n\n**4. \"Regularized Langevin Dynamics for Combinatorial Optimization\" (Recent)**\n\nThe iSCO (Sun et al., 2023) variant achieves state-of-the-art performance on various combinatorial optimization benchmarks, and discrete Langevin samplers assign gradient-informed change probabilities to all coordinates in parallel.\n\n**5. \"Reheated Gradient-based Discrete Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\" (2025)**\n\nRecently, gradient-based discrete sampling has emerged as a highly efficient solver for combinatorial optimization, but researchers identified a critical issue termed \"wandering in contours\" where algorithms sample different solutions with similar objective values, and introduced a reheating mechanism inspired by physics concepts to overcome this limitation.\n\nThese papers represent the cutting edge of research on combining discrete MCMC methods with gradient-based sampling for solving combinatorial optimization problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"564c5c06-a401-4290-a886-d1189f1632ea","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:10:42.246Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9af120b8-0ebc-4e9f-9fe6-005d3d8f8121","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"564c5c06-a401-4290-a886-d1189f1632ea","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a2d29f537e2b0ba30","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RTcn4vN8iyNfcZP6vvUZqx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough material. Let me synthesize into the requested ≤800-word brief.\n\n---\n\n# MCMC / CEM / SMC / 并行回火 调研简报（2024-2026）\n\n## 1. MCMC for Combinatorial Sampling\n\n**主线进展是 gradient-based discrete MCMC**——传统 MCMC 在离散组合空间靠局部翻转，混合慢；2023 之后的新一代利用目标函数对离散变量的\"伪梯度\"（Gibbs-with-Gradients、Discrete Langevin、iSCO）做并行化提议。\n\n代表工作：\n- **Sun et al., ICML 2023, \"Revisiting Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization\"**：表明现代 MCMC 在 MaxCut/MIS/SAT 上能跟 SOTA 启发式打平。\n- **iSCO (Discrete Langevin)**：在多个 CO benchmark 上 SOTA。\n- **Reheated Gradient-based Discrete Sampling (arXiv 2503.04047, 2025)**：发现\"wandering in contours\"问题（在等高线上瞎逛），引入\"reheat\"机制突破。\n- **Regularized Langevin Dynamics (arXiv 2502.00277, 2025)**：连续松弛 + 正则化，把 CO 变成可微分采样。\n\n对 SAT/CSP：hybrid MCMC（SAT 求解器做 proposal + Metropolis 单变量翻转修正）能保证 stationary 分布正确，2024-2025 在 SAT-sampling for statistical testing 上有应用。\n\n## 2. Cross-Entropy Method (CEM)\n\nRubinstein 1997 起源的方法，2024-2026 没有重大原创性突破，但**作为子模块嵌入 neural CO** 很常见：\n- **CEM 训练 NCO (Neural CO) 模型**：每 epoch 采样 N 个解，取精英分位数 (top-ρ%) 当伪标签自模仿。这就是\"sample without replacement, but improvement\"那条线（arXiv 2403.15180）。\n- **CEM-RL (ICLR 2019, 至今活跃)**：CEM 演化 + TD3 梯度，在 policy search 上一致优于纯 evolutionary 或纯 RL。\n- 局限：**样本量需求大**，real-time planning 不友好（Pinneri et al. 已论证）。\n\n## 3. Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) for Optimization\n\n- **Duan (2019) SMC for CO**：把 CO 目标视作非归一化概率密度 π(x) ∝ exp(βf(x))，β 渐进升温（density-tempering），粒子群带权重重采样。**有收敛证明**，不是元启发式。\n- 2024-2025 主要应用在 **POMDP policy optimization**（SPO, arXiv 2402.07963 / 2505.16732），不是直接 CO。\n- **SMC-SA**（Zhou & Chen）：把模拟退火嵌进 SMC 框架，温度调度更鲁棒。\n\nSMC 跟 CEM 区别：CEM 显式拟合参数族（如 Bernoulli 边缘），SMC 用粒子近似分布，不假设参数族。\n\n## 4. Replica Exchange / Parallel Tempering (PT)\n\n**这块 2025 最热**，值得重点关注：\n- **IsingFormer (arXiv 2509.23043, 2025)**：Transformer 学习目标分布，生成\"全局 proposal\"插入 PT 的 Metropolis 步。在 3D spin glass（NP-hard）上找到显著更低的能量。\n- **2D Parallel Tempering (arXiv 2506.14781, 2025)**：除温度维外加约束维，**专门处理硬约束**。\n- **Generalised Parallel Tempering / GePT (arXiv 2502.10328, 2025)**：把 normalizing flow / diffusion 当 replica 之间的 transport，跨能量壁垒更高效。\n- **Permutational Boltzmann Machine + PT**：在 QAP 上比 SOTA 快 100×。\n\n## 5. SAT/CSP/Placement 应用现状\n\n- SAT：hybrid MCMC（SAT proposal + 单翻转）已是主流采样路径\n- QAP（二次分配，跟 placement 同构）：PT-based Boltzmann machine 显著领先\n- Placement：直接把 CO 写成 Ising/QUBO，PT 或 discrete Langevin 求解\n- 但**多数工作在无 hard constraint 或软约束场景**，对 CP-SAT 风格的硬约束 + 硬可行性证明不友好\n\n## 6. 跟 RL / GP / LLM evolution 对比\n\n| 方法 | 探索强度 | 样本效率 | 硬约束友好 | 收敛证明 | 2025 热度 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CEM | 中 | 低 | 弱 | 启发式 | 平 |\n| Discrete MCMC (iSCO) | 高 | 中 | 弱（需软化） | 渐近 | 高 |\n| SMC | 高 | 中 | 中 | 有 | 中 |\n| Parallel Tempering | 高 | 中-高 | 中 | 渐近 | **很高** |\n| RL (PPO/POMO) | 中 | 中 | 中 | 无 | 高 |\n| GP / 演化 | 中 | 低 | 弱 | 无 | 平 |\n| LLM evolution (FunSearch/AlphaEvolve) | 高（语义层） | 极低 | 强（生成代码可带约束） | 无 | 极高 |\n\n关键洞察：**MCMC/CEM/SMC 这一系跟你的设定有 mismatch**——它们假设可以频繁评估 f(x) 的\"软\"近似，而你的 candidate 评估是黑盒的\"完整 Benders 跑一遍\"，单次评估就要数秒到数分钟。\n\n## 是否值得用作 candidate ordering\n\n**部分有用，但不直接拿现成的 CEM/MCMC 来做 ordering，而是借鉴其思想**：\n\n**有价值的迁移**：\n1. **CEM 的精英参数族更新思路**：把 candidate 的离散特征（如 ghost rectangle 位置/形状/朝向）维护一组 Bernoulli/Categorical 边缘分布，每 wave 用 top-ρ% 解过的 candidate 更新边缘。比单纯的 banditscore-greedy 多一层概率正则化，**抗噪声更好**——你的 UNKNOWN/UNPROVEN 标签本身就是噪声。\n2. **Parallel Tempering 的多温度并行**：你已经有 `--parallel-processes 4` 多进程，**让不同进程跑不同\"温度\"**（即不同 ordering 探索强度：贪心 / 中度随机 / 高度随机），周期性交换\"成功 candidate\"。零成本扩展，2025 PT 在硬约束下证明有效。\n3. **SMC 的 density tempering**：β 从小到大渐进收紧——实践对应 wave 早期允许低分 candidate，后期收敛到高分。比固定阈值更鲁棒。\n\n**不建议直接套**：\n- 纯 CEM 需大样本，你的 wave 预算太小\n- iSCO/discrete Langevin 需要 f(x) 可微近似，你没有\n- IsingFormer 那种学习 proposal 的，得自建 Transformer + 你的数据规模也不够（266 实例）\n\n**结论：值得，但作为\"候选排序的概率包装层 + 多温度并行调度\"思路借鉴，不作为独立 lane。可以并入 Lane B（deterministic tuning）的子模块或 Lane C（AI sidecar）的 hint 生成器，AI Safety Contract 友好（order_only / hint-only，不写 checkpoint）。优先级中等，低于 PT-启发的多温度并行调度（这个几乎零成本就能上）。**\n\nSources:\n- [Revisiting Sampling for Combinatorial Optimization (Sun et al., ICML 2023)](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/sun23c.html)\n- [Reheated Gradient-based Discrete Sampling for CO (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2503.04047)\n- [Regularized Langevin Dynamics for CO (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.00277)\n- [IsingFormer: PT with Learned Proposals (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.23043v1)\n- [Two-dimensional Parallel Tempering for Constrained Optimization (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14781v2)\n- [Generalised Parallel Tempering via Flows and Diffusions (2025)](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.10328v1)\n- [Self-Improvement for Neural CO (CEM-style, 2024)](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.15180v1)\n- [SMC for Policy Optimisation in POMDPs (2025)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.16732)\n- [Sequential Monte Carlo Optimization and Statistical Inference (Duan, 2023)](https://wires.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/wics.1598)\n- [CEM-RL (ICLR 2019, baseline)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.01222)\n- [Cross-Entropy Method (Rubinstein & Kroese, foundational)](https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4321-0)\n- [Permutational Boltzmann Machine with PT for QAP](https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-58112-1_22)"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1423,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3201,"cache_read_input_tokens":26739,"output_tokens":3377,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3201},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1423,"output_tokens":3377,"cache_read_input_tokens":26739,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoyTHXcVtoJZqgPhLESP","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"96688f15-887e-4237-9888-41facea2daea","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:11:44.612Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
